# Quiz Arena — C++ Coding Standards and Project Constraints

## Purpose

This file defines the coding conventions and technical constraints that Cursor must follow when modifying the Quiz Arena project.

The rules are based on the project requirements and the C++ conventions used in the course. New language features or libraries should not be introduced without prior approval.

## How These Rules Were Developed

These rules were added and refined throughout development based on issues found while reviewing Cursor's proposed changes.

---

## 1. Required Coding Restrictions

| Not Allowed | Accepted Alternative |
|-------------|----------------------|
| `auto` | Explicit types: `int`, `double`, `Stock*`, `const string&`, `Question*`, etc. |
| `override` | Redeclare the virtual function with the **same signature**; do **not** write the `override` keyword |
| `unique_ptr` | Raw pointer + `new` / `delete` |
| `auto_ptr` | Raw pointer + `new` / `delete` |
| `shared_ptr`, `weak_ptr`, any smart pointer | Manual memory only |
| `#include <memory>` for smart pointers | Not for project code |

```cpp
// Not Allowed
auto x = 5;
void ask() override;
unique_ptr<Question> p;

// Accepted Alternative
int x = 5;
void ask();              // virtual in base; same signature in derived
Question* p = new MultipleChoiceQuestion(...);
delete p;
```

---

## 2. Not Allowed — C++ Language Constraints

| Not Allowed | Notes |
|-------------|-------|
| `#pragma once` | Use `#ifndef` / `#define` / `#endif` only |
| `reinterpret_cast` | Not in normal project code |
| C-style cast `(Type*)ptr` | Prefer `static_cast` / `dynamic_cast` if a cast is needed |
| `static_cast` for unsafe runtime downcast | Prefer `dynamic_cast` for polymorphic downcast |
| `#define` macros for business logic | e.g. `#define min(a,b)` — use function templates instead. Macros OK only for include guards / `#ifdef DEBUG` |
| Template class method bodies only in `.cpp` | Class template implementations must be in `.h` |
| Calling constructor manually | Constructors run automatically |
| Calling destructor manually | Destructors run automatically (except defining a pure virtual destructor body) |
| Instantiating an abstract class | `Base b;` when base has pure virtual — illegal |
| Shallow copy of `char*` / dynamic memory | `m_name = other.m_name` in copy constructor — not allowed |
| Exceptions for normal / frequent control flow | Prefer `bool` when that is enough |
| Operator overloading with unclear meaning | Only when meaning is obvious |
| Defining new operator symbols | Cannot invent operators |
| Changing operator precedence or operand count | Language rules are fixed |
| Overloading: `::`, `.*`, `.`, `?:`, `sizeof`, `typeid` | These six cannot be overloaded |
| Accessing base `private` from derived | Use `protected` in base if derived needs access |
| `delete` through `Base*` without `virtual ~Base()` | Undefined behavior / leaks |
| Modifying through a `const` reference | Only `const` methods on `const` objects |
| Binding non-const reference / non-const pointer to const | Const-correctness violation |

---

## 3. Not Allowed — Unsafe Patterns

| Not Allowed | Notes |
|-------------|-------|
| `goto` | Not used in course style |
| Leaving `new` without matching `delete` | Memory leak |
| `delete` on a non-owning pointer | Only the owner deletes |
| Uninitialized local variables | Undefined / garbage values |
| Aliasing external C-strings without deep copy | Must `new[]` + `strcpy` |
| Skipping bounds check before index access | Out of range |
| Skipping `nullptr` check before dereference | Crash risk |
| `delete` vs `delete[]` mismatch | `new`→`delete`, `new[]`→`delete[]` |
| Double delete | Set pointer to `nullptr` after free when resetting |
| Self-assignment without `if (this != &other)` | Corrupts object |
| Global variables for game state | State lives in objects |
| Mixing `cin`/`cout` into entity / core logic classes | Separate UI layer |
| Graphics libraries (SFML, SDL, etc.) | Terminal-only game |
| Third-party libraries | Not allowed |

---

## 4. Not Allowed — Features Not Approved for This Project

Do **not** use unless explicitly approved:

- `constexpr`, `decltype`, `enum class`
- `std::optional`, `std::variant`, `std::any`
- Lambdas: `[](...) { }`
- Range-based `for` that relies on `auto` (e.g. `for (auto x : v)`)
- `NULL` instead of `nullptr` (project uses `nullptr`)
- Structured bindings, `using enum`
- `std::thread`, async, concurrency
- `printf` as default I/O style (project uses `iostream`)

