## 7. Required — Memory & Ownership

- Every `new` / `new[]` has matching `delete` / `delete[]`
- After freeing when resetting an object: set pointer to `nullptr`
- Check `ptr == nullptr` before dereference
- Deep copy for owned `char*` / dynamic arrays in copy constructor and `operator=`
- Self-assignment guard: `if (this != &other)`
- In assignment: free old buffer before allocating new
- Class with pointer member → **Rule of Three**: copy constructor + destructor + `operator=`
- Non-owning pointer: store it, never `delete` it
- Whoever allocates owns and frees

```cpp
// Deep copy C-string
m_name = new char[strlen(name) + 1];
strcpy(m_name, name);

// Free
delete[] m_name;
m_name = nullptr;

// Assignment
ClassName& ClassName::operator=(const ClassName& other) {
    if (this != &other) {
        // free old, deep-copy new
    }
    return *this;
}
```

---

## 8. Required — Validation & Error Handling

**nullptr / bounds (always when applicable):**
- Function receives `T*` that may be null → early `return false` / `return`
- Before `m_data[i]` → confirm `m_data != nullptr` and valid index
- Stack push/pop → check not full / not empty
- Reject negative sizes, zero durations, invalid amounts as appropriate

**Return `bool` when an operation can fail locally** (preferred for expected failures):
- insert / get / search / push / pop / save failure that can be handled → `true`/`false`

**Use exceptions when:**
- Constructor cannot build a valid object
- `operator[]` has no valid return for bad index → `throw out_of_range` (or custom exception inheriting `exception`)
- Do **not** use exceptions for normal expected loop/control flow

**Compare pattern (when used):** `compare()` returns `-1` / `0` / `1`

**Tests:** `assert(condition)`; wrap test `main` in `try` / `catch (const exception& e)` when appropriate

